From d5911b497815fbafd43f63aa32b9739dcc5f550f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slopdealer <326519127+slopdealer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:54:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/49] R1 tests: randomized schedules, verdicts, request
 counts, settle delay

lock_wait_schedule_test.go: two or three processes (backup, restore, prune,
forget, forget --prune, check) on the in-memory backend with per-operation
delays from a seeded generator, in virtual time (testing/synctest). Every
schedule runs in stock order and with the tree's wait work on the same lock
timeline. Oracle: after a confirmed lock a command uses no snapshot or index
file that was removed before, misses none that existed (unless a
non-conflicting process wrote it after our lock file), ends as in stock order,
and the repository ends up readable and equal to the stock-order run's; no
goroutine is left. The stock-order run is held to the same oracle.
500 schedules by default (about 15 s); RESTIC_R1_SCHEDULES, _SEED, _VERBOSE.
RESTIC_R1_SCHEDULE_LAG models a backend that lists new lock files late
(uniform, per file, per file with listings that are not snapshots), with
schedules aimed at that window.

wait_index_load_validate_test.go: the three verdicts of the comparison. Keep:
two listings, nothing loaded. An index file added by a backup beside us: that
file is loaded and no other. Index files replaced, a snapshot removed, or a
failed read during the wait: everything is read again, snapshots first. In
every case the command holds what a fresh handle reads.

lock_wait_requests_test.go: backend operations of one backup, restore, ls,
check, forget, forget --prune and prune, by kind and file type, in stock
order and with the wait work; optional per-request latency
(RESTIC_R1_REQUEST_LATENCY) with wall times.

lock_wait_duration_test.go: the confirmation scan starts exactly one settle
delay after the lock file was saved in every path (wait work early, running,
failing, hanging; cancellation; a conflicting lock 1 ms before the end), and
no goroutine, lock file or prepared state outlives a command or a refused
attempt.

